## Files Delivered

```
api/
  status.js          ← GET /api/status        — checks connected + follow status
  verify-tweet.js    ← GET /api/verify-tweet  — scans recent tweets for required text
  form.js            ← GET /api/form          — protected Google Form redirect (updated)

public/
  whitelist.js       ← Frontend auto-verification engine (include in index.html)
  whitelist-section.html  ← Drop-in HTML for the whitelist section (IDs wired to whitelist.js)

vercel.json          ← Updated with /api/verify-tweet route
```

---

## Integration into Your index.html

1. **Replace** your existing whitelist section HTML with the contents of `whitelist-section.html`
2. Place `whitelist.js` in your `public/` folder
3. The `<script src="/whitelist.js"></script>` tag at the bottom of the section loads it automatically
4. Your existing CSS from the provided stylesheet handles all styling — no changes needed

---

## Notes

- The Twitter OAuth callback (`/api/callback.js`) must set the `x_user` cookie with `{ accessToken, userId, username }` 
  so that `status.js` and `verify-tweet.js` can read it.
- `tweetVerified` is written back into the cookie by `verify-tweet.js` when the tweet is found.
- `isFollowing` is **not** persisted in the cookie — it's always checked live. 
  If you want to persist it (to avoid API calls), update `status.js` to write it back similarly to `verify-tweet.js`.
- Twitter API v2 free tier allows 500,000 tweet reads/month and 500 follow lookups/month. 
  At 1,111 whitelist applicants with max 36 polls each, you're well within limits.